// boot.hex -- minimal reset-vector smoke test for sim/rtl/axi_sim_mem.v's
// 128KiB ROM (mapped at 0xE0000-0xFFFFF).
//
// The first real run of this testbench showed the core issuing a steady,
// unbroken sequence of code-fetch (AR) bursts starting at physical
// address 0xFFC00 and incrementing by 0x10 (one 128-bit fetch line)
// every 143 cycles, for as long as the run lasted -- i.e. it was
// executing straight through the zero-filled ROM (0x00 0x00 decodes as
// "ADD [bx+si], al", a harmless 2-byte fall-through). 0xFFC00-0xFFFFF is
// exactly 1KB, matching soc_rtl/axi_rom.v's original 1KB boot ROM size,
// so 0xFFC00 (top of the 1MB real-mode address space minus 1KB) looks
// like this core's actual reset fetch address -- not the classic
// 8086-style 0xFFFF0 assumed in an earlier version of this file.
//
// ROM offsets (ROM_BASE = 0xE0000): 0xFFC00 -> 0x1FC00, 0xFFFF0 -> 0x1FFF0.
//
// Program: an infinite self-jump (JMP $-2 = EB FE) placed at both
// candidate addresses, so a successful boot is observable as the
// code-fetch address converging and staying fixed, regardless of which
// hypothesis turns out to be right. Intentionally the simplest possible
// test -- only exercises reset, instruction fetch, decode, and a
// relative branch; no assumption about default data/stack segment
// bases, which a memory-touching test would need.
@1fc00
EB FE
@1fff0
EB FE
